_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 32
---
order: 32
---
short_label: VI.32
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If two triangles having two sides proportional to two sides are placed together at one angle so that their corresponding sides are also parallel, then the remaining sides of the triangles are in a straight line.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|ABCt} and {DCE|DCEt} be two triangles having the two sides {AB} and {AC} proportional to the two sides {DC} and {DE}, so that {AB} is to {AC} as {DC} is to {DE}, and {AB} parallel to {DC}, and {AC} parallel to {DE}.

I say that {BC} is in a straight line with {CE}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="370" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.32 diagram" src="propVI32.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure draws the two filled triangles ABC and CDE, placed
    // together at C with AB parallel to DC and AC parallel to DE (D slides
    // on the parallel to AB through C; E is set by the similarity).
    //
    // Everything added is transparent (;0;0;0 lines, ;0;0;0;0 polygons) or
    // an angle marker (hidden at rest), so the resting figure is unchanged:
    // the sides AB, AC, BC, DC, DE, CE, and faceless twins ABCt / DCEt of
    // the filled triangles (a highlighted filled polygon is raised over the
    // markers inside it, #140), reached as {ABC|ABCt} and {DCE|DCEt}. The
    // angle names collide with the triangles, so every angle ref uses a
    // display override onto its marker.
    //
    // Markers: BAC = ACD = CDE share one colour, ABC = DCE another. ACE is
